    <statement><p>Use the Fundamental Theorem of Calculus to find</p>
<p><m>\displaystyle  \int_{3\pi}^{3\pi/2}\, e^{-\cos\mathopen{}\left(q\right)}\cdot \sin\mathopen{}\left(q\right) \,dq =</m> <fillin characters="35" name="AnSwEr0001"/></p>
</statement>
    <answer>
      <p>
        <m>-1.71828182845905</m>
      </p>
    </answer>
    <solution><m>w = -\cos\mathopen{}\left(q\right)</m>, so that <m>dw = \sin\mathopen{}\left(q\right)\,dq</m>.
Then <m>w(3\pi) = 1</m> and <m>w(3\pi/2) = 0</m>, so that
<me>\int_{3\pi}^{3\pi/2}\, e^{-\cos\mathopen{}\left(q\right)}\cdot \sin\mathopen{}\left(q\right) \,dq = 
  \int_1^0\,e^w\,dw = e^w\bigg|_1^0 = 1 - e.</me>




</solution>
